<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>5.15.4: Comparing Linear and Exponential Growth</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01523</md:content-id>
    <md:title>5.15.4: Comparing Linear and Exponential Growth</md:title>
    <md:uuid>5b9bd6ee-5dc9-4d89-8e28-e1c24e9324c2</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section>
      <title> Cool Down Activity</title>
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para> The linear function \(g\) is defined by \(g(x)=4x\). 
        
     </para>
          <para> Show that \(g(3)\) and \(g(2)\) have a difference of 4.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para/>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <list>
            <item>\(
        g(3) = 4\cdot 3 = 12\), and \(g(2) = 4 \cdot 2 = 8\). The difference of 12 and 8 is 4.</item>
            <item>\(g(3) − g(2) = 4 \cdot (3) − 4 \cdot (2) = 4 \cdot (3−2) = 4\)</item>
          </list>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para> Show that when the input value increases from \(x\) to \(x+1\), the output values \(g(x+1)\) and \(g(x)\) have a difference of 4. Use the '^' symbol to enter an exponent.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para/>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para> \(g(x+1) − g(x) = 4 \cdot (x+1) − 4x = 4 \cdot (x+1−x) = 4 \cdot (1)\), which equals 4.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para> The exponential function \(h\) is defined by \(h(x)=4^x\). 
        
     </para>
          <para> Show that \(h(3)\) and \(h(2)\) have a quotient of 4. Use the '^' symbol to enter an exponent.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para/>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <list>
            <item>\(h(3) = 4^3= 64\), and \(h(2) = 4^2 = 16\). The quotient is \(\frac{64}{16}\), which is 4.</item>
            <item>\(\frac{
        h(3)}{h(2)}=\frac {4^3}{4^2}=4^{3−2} = 4\)</item>
          </list>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para> Show that when the input value increases from \(x\) to \(x+1\), the output values \(h(x+1)\) and \(h(x)\) have a quotient of 4.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para/>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para> \(\frac {h(x+1)}{h(x)}=\frac{4^{x+1}}{4^x}=4^{x+1−x} = 4^1\), which equals 4.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <note id="note-00001">
        <para>Why Should I Care?</para>
        <figure id="fig-00001">
          <media alt="Insert Alt Text">
            <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Algebra-1_05_17_09_01.png"/>
          </media>
        </figure>
        <para>Exponential models can help you understand the power of investment. After two years, this investor made $210 off of an initial investment of $1000 because of the 10% interest rate.</para>
        <para>Can you calculate how much money the investor will have made after twenty years?</para>
      </note>
    </section>
  </content>
